1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,929
How did a conqueror feared

2
00:00:01,929 --> 00:00:03,858
across continents become the spark

3
00:00:03,858 --> 00:00:05,400
of Samarkand’s golden age?

4
00:00:05,400 --> 00:00:07,740
Amir Temur forged an empire

5
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:10,080
across Central Asia, Persia, and

6
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,951
Afghanistan through relentless campaigns.

7
00:00:11,951 --> 00:00:13,991
His armies made him one

8
00:00:13,991 --> 00:00:16,031
of history’s most formidable commanders,

9
00:00:16,031 --> 00:00:18,071
though conquest came at terrible

10
00:00:18,071 --> 00:00:18,887
human cost.

11
00:00:18,887 --> 00:00:22,110
Yet Temur also gathered artists, poets,

12
00:00:22,110 --> 00:00:25,333
and scholars around his dazzling capital,

13
00:00:25,333 --> 00:00:25,871
Samarkand.

14
00:00:25,871 --> 00:00:28,235
At Damascus, he met historian

15
00:00:28,235 --> 00:00:30,599
Ibn Khaldun, a striking encounter

16
00:00:30,599 --> 00:00:32,489
between conqueror and chronicler.

17
00:00:32,489 --> 00:00:35,513
Timurid patronage helped nourish a Renaissance

18
00:00:35,513 --> 00:00:38,033
of architecture, art, and learning.

19
00:00:38,033 --> 00:00:40,195
So was Temur only a destroyer—or

20
00:00:40,195 --> 00:00:42,357
the ruler whose empire made Samarkand

21
00:00:42,357 --> 00:00:42,718
shine?
